1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,064
Start with immediate threats. A long symptom interview must not delay
basic assessment or urgent care. Once that first assessment is under
way, gather symptom history and ECG evidence alongside vital signs,
skin and mentation, using available team members and the authorized
workflow.

2
00:00:20,064 --> 00:00:37,200
The school integration sequence is an educational checklist, not a
command to finish history before checking an unstable patient’s
circulation. Synthesize the information repeatedly. A patient can
change while you are measuring an interval or completing the next part
of the story.

3
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:56,664
Ask site and onset, then let the patient describe character and
radiation in their own words. Record the actual onset time or its
uncertainty. Ask about associated breathlessness, nausea, sweating,
palpitations or fainting rather than limiting the interview to a pain
score.

4
00:00:56,664 --> 00:01:16,872
Pressure-like central discomfort and radiation can increase concern,
but no single description rules in or rules out ACS. Sharp,
reproducible, abdominal or gradually developing symptoms can still
require urgent evaluation in context. Keep dangerous noncoronary
alternatives in view.

5
00:01:16,872 --> 00:01:35,376
Complete SOCRATES with duration and recurrence, what worsens or
relieves the symptom, and the patient’s rated severity. Ask whether
this differs from previous episodes and how it affects activity. A
timeline helps relate symptoms to serial ECGs and physiological
change.

6
00:01:35,376 --> 00:01:57,528
Do not use brief duration, gradual onset, relief with rest or response
to a medicine as a stand-alone exclusion or confirmation of ACS.
Severe symptoms may be urgent, but low reported severity can also
accompany serious illness. Document the patient’s description rather
than rewriting it to match a stereotype.

7
00:01:57,528 --> 00:02:19,032
Hypovolemic shock reduces filling through blood or fluid loss.
Distributive shock changes vascular tone and distribution, as in
sepsis or anaphylaxis. Cardiogenic shock reflects inadequate pump
performance, while obstructive shock involves a barrier such as
pulmonary embolism, tamponade or tension pneumothorax.

8
00:02:19,032 --> 00:02:40,728
The ECG may show sinus tachycardia, nonspecific changes, ischemia or
an arrhythmia in several of these states. A pattern of right-heart
strain or low voltage is not a stand-alone diagnosis of obstruction.
History, physical findings and serial physiology determine which
mechanism is plausible and what help is needed.

9
00:02:40,728 --> 00:03:02,208
The school pairs ST change with hypotension, nonspecific ECG with
instability, normal ECG with persistent concerning symptoms, and
subtle T changes with nonclassic symptoms. In each, the point is to
combine evidence rather than allow one reassuring or alarming feature
to dominate everything.

10
00:03:02,208 --> 00:03:23,040
Use an appropriate ACS pathway when warranted, but keep other causes
of deterioration active. Oxygen is given for its indication, not
automatically for any ST label. Medications, fluids and advanced
procedures require their own assessment, contraindication review and
provider authority.

11
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:44,328
In this original case, an adult becomes sweaty and confused after
exertional upper-abdominal pressure. A clean monitoring strip shows
regular sinus activity, but the pulse is weak and pressure is falling.
State the problem as impaired perfusion with a possible ischemic
presentation, not simply normal sinus rhythm.

12
00:03:44,328 --> 00:04:07,272
Prioritize immediate assessment and support through the authorized
pathway, obtain a diagnostic ECG efficiently and communicate
deterioration early. Consider cardiac and noncardiac causes. Reassess
whether symptoms, mentation, pulse and pressure change together; a
stable electrical rate does not mean the patient has stabilized.

13
00:04:07,272 --> 00:04:27,552
A concise handover states the main concern and onset, relevant
history, current and previous physiological findings, ECG observations
and technical limits. Then describe what was done, the response and
the support needed. Make clear which findings were measured, reported
or inferred.

14
00:04:27,552 --> 00:04:47,520
For the original case, report persistent poor perfusion despite an
organized sinus strip, whether the diagnostic ECG is available, and
the trend after care. Do not claim excluded ACS, confirmed shock
subtype or successful stabilization unless the evidence actually
supports that conclusion.

15
00:04:47,520 --> 00:05:10,776
You have now worked through the fifteen teaching chapters: anatomy,
electrical foundations, measurement, leads, systematic interpretation,
rhythm families, repolarization, blocks, physiology, artifact,
instability and integration. Use the bilingual reading and original
practice to explain decisions aloud, not just recognize headings.

16
00:05:10,776 --> 00:05:30,936
School assessment and supervised practical requirements remain
separate. This independent classroom neither answers the school Quiz
nor submits completion or certification. Review the assigned Week
Three textbook reading already provided, and use the school’s normal
pathway for its own learning and evaluation requirements.
